        <div role="img" aria-label="Line chart showing temperature anomalies from 1700 to present. The 1850-1900 baseline is ~0.5C above the 1700 baseline, meaning true warming has surpassed 1.5C.">
          <div class="chart-container">
            <canvas id="timelineChart"></canvas>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="split-content animate delay-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h2>Why Sclerosponges?</h2>
          <p>Sclerosponges are long-lived marine organisms found in tropical and subtropical oceans. As they grow, strontium-to-calcium (Sr/Ca) ratios in their aragonite skeletons faithfully track ambient water temperature.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Continuous growth</strong> over centuries, yielding unbroken annual-resolution records back to ~1700 CE.</li>
            <li><strong>Tropical ocean sensitivity</strong> — Caribbean sites capture large-scale Sea Surface Temperature (SST) variability.</li>
            <li><strong>Independence</strong> from instrumental networks, which only begin around 1850.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h2>Policy & Climate Targets</h2>
          <p>The IPCC's 1850–1900 reference period already contained ~0.5 °C of anthropogenic warming.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The Paris Agreement's 1.5 °C target is framed relative to "pre-industrial levels," meaning <strong>this threshold has already been surpassed</strong> when using a true pre-industrial baseline (1700–1790).</li>
            <li>Adopting this baseline reduces the remaining carbon budget for 1.5 °C to effectively zero.</li>
            <li>The 2.0 °C guardrail is closer than previously estimated, with only ~0.3 °C of additional warming available.</li>
